  "answer_text": "Great question \u2014 and it's genuinely debated! Here's a breakdown of the main pronunciations:\n\n---\n\n## How to Pronounce \"Doge\" \ud83d\udc15\n\nAccording to Wikipedia, the meme can be pronounced **\"dohzh\"** (/do\u028a\u0292/), **\"dohj\"** (/do\u028ad\u0292/), or **\"dohg\"** (/do\u028a\u0261/).\n\nHere are the main camps:\n\n### 1. \ud83c\udfc6 \"Dohzh\" or \"Dohj\" \u2014 The Most Common / \"Official\"\nThe pronunciation **\"dohj\"** (written as /do\u028a\u0292/ in IPA) was confirmed by Dogecoin's co-creator Jackson Palmer, who said the name was inspired by the animated series *Homestar Runner*, where a character spells out D-O-G-E and another character reads it as \"dohj.\"\n\n### 2. \"Dohg\" \u2014 Like \"Vogue\" or \"Rogue\"\nSome people pronounce it **\"dohg\"** with a long O, hard G, and silent E \u2014 reasoning that the original word \"dog\" has a hard G, so the E only modifies the vowel, not the consonant \u2014 sort of like how \"slop\" becomes \"slope\" and \"mop\" becomes \"mope.\"\n\n### 3. \"Doh-geh\" \u2014 Like \"Doggy\" but fancier\nThere's also **\"doh-geh\"**, with a long O, hard G, and a spoken \"e\" sound at the end (like \"ballet\"), preferred by some because it sounds like a bastardized version of \"doggy.\"\n\n### 4. The Historical \"Doge\" (Venice)\nThe word \"doge\" also refers to the chief magistrate of Venice, and some people use that older pronunciation, which feels more established and authoritative.\n\n---\n\n## The Bottom Line\n\nSince \"Doge\" is a word that was practically made up for the purposes of an internet meme, it's no surprise that there's a lot of confusion about how to pronounce it. That said, **\"dohj\"** (rhymes with \"dodge\") is the most widely accepted pronunciation, backed by the creators of Dogecoin and the *Homestar Runner* reference that inspired the meme. Think of it like the GIF debate \u2014 there's no single universally agreed-upon answer, but \"dohj\" is your safest bet!",
